EN = {
22: "Climbing the staircase that led to Petrovich, which, to do it justice, was all smeared with water and slops and thoroughly permeated with that spirituous smell that eats the eyes and is, as is well known, inseparably present on all the back staircases of Petersburg houses, climbing the staircase, Akaky Akakievich was already wondering how much Petrovich would ask, and mentally resolved not to give more than two roubles. The door was open, because the landlady, cooking some fish, had let so much smoke into the kitchen that you could not see even the cockroaches. Akaky Akakievich passed through the kitchen, not noticed even by the landlady herself, and at last entered the room, where he saw Petrovich sitting on a wide, unpainted wooden table with his legs tucked under him like a Turkish pasha. His feet, as is the custom of tailors sitting at work, were bare.",
23: "And first of all there caught the eye the big toe, very familiar to Akaky Akakievich, with a sort of mangled nail, thick and strong as a tortoise's shell. Round Petrovich's neck hung a skein of silk and thread, and on his knees lay some rag. For some three minutes already he had been trying to thread a needle, had missed, and was therefore very angry at the darkness and even at the thread itself, muttering under his breath: {P:“It won't go in, the barbarian; you have worn me out, you rogue!”} Akaky Akakievich was unhappy that he had come just at the moment when Petrovich was angry: he liked to order something from Petrovich when the latter was already somewhat in high spirits, or, as his wife put it, “primed with raw vodka, the one-eyed devil.” In that state Petrovich usually gave in very readily and agreed, and every time even bowed and thanked.",
24: "Afterwards, it is true, his wife would come, complaining that her husband had been drunk and so had taken the work too cheap; but you would add a ten-kopeck piece, and the thing was done. Now, however, Petrovich seemed to be sober, and therefore stern, unyielding and fond of asking the devil knows what prices. Akaky Akakievich saw this and was about to retreat, as the saying goes, but the thing was already begun. Petrovich screwed up his one eye at him very intently, and Akaky Akakievich said involuntarily: {A:“Good day, Petrovich!”}",
25: "{P:“I wish you good health, sir,”} said Petrovich, and squinted his eye at Akaky Akakievich's hands, wishing to spy out what sort of prey he was bringing.",
26: "{A:“I have come to you, Petrovich, about... you know...”} It must be known that Akaky Akakievich expressed himself for the most part in prepositions, adverbs and, finally, such particles as have absolutely no meaning. And if the matter was very difficult, he even had the habit of not finishing his sentence at all, so that quite often, having begun his speech with the words: {A:“This really is quite, you know...”} then there was nothing more, and he himself forgot, thinking that he had said everything.",
27: "{P:“What is it, then?”} said Petrovich, and at the same time looked over his whole uniform coat with his single eye, from the collar to the sleeves, the back, the tails and the buttonholes, all of which was very familiar to him, because it was his own work. It is the custom with tailors; it is the first thing he does on meeting.",
28: "{A:“Well, it is like this, Petrovich... the overcoat, the cloth... you see, everywhere else it is quite sound, it has got a little dusty and looks as if it were old, but it is new, and only in one place it is a little, you know... on the back, and then on one shoulder it is a little worn through, and on this shoulder a little, you see, that's all. And not much work...”}",
29: "Petrovich took the dressing gown, spread it out first on the table, examined it for a long time, shook his head and reached with his hand to the window for a round snuffbox with the portrait of some general, which general exactly is not known, because the place where the face had been was poked through with a finger and then pasted over with a square scrap of paper. Having taken a pinch of snuff, Petrovich held the coat up on his hands and examined it against the light, and again shook his head. Then he turned it lining upwards and shook his head again, again took off the lid with the general pasted over with paper, and, having stuffed tobacco up his nose, closed it, put the snuffbox away and finally said:",
30: "{P:“No, it can't be mended: a wretched wardrobe!”}",
31: "At these words Akaky Akakievich's heart sank. {A:“Why can't it, Petrovich?”} he said in an almost pleading, childlike voice. {A:“It is only that it is worn at the shoulders; surely you have some little pieces...”}",
32: "{P:“Little pieces can be found, little pieces will turn up,”} said Petrovich, {P:“but to sew them on is impossible: the stuff is quite rotten, touch it with a needle and there it goes creeping away.”}",
33: "{A:“Let it creep, and you put a patch on at once.”}",
34: "{P:“But there is nothing to put a patch on, nothing for it to hold to, the support is far too weak. It is only called cloth; blow a wind and it will fly apart.”}",
35: "{A:“Well, fasten it anyhow. Really, how can it be like this!..”}",
36: "{P:“No,”} said Petrovich firmly, {P:“nothing can be done. It is quite a bad business. You had better, when the cold winter weather comes, make foot-wrappings of it, because stockings are not warm. The Germans invented them to take more money for themselves”} (Petrovich liked, when the occasion arose, to take a dig at the Germans), {P:“and as for an overcoat, it seems you will have to have a new one made.”}",
37: "At the word “new” Akaky Akakievich's eyes clouded over, and everything in the room began to swim before him. He saw clearly only the general with the paper-covered face on the lid of Petrovich's snuffbox. {A:“How, a new one?”} he said, still as if in a dream. {A:“I have no money for it.”}",
38: "{P:“Yes, a new one,”} said Petrovich with barbarous calm.",
39: "{A:“Well, and if I had to have a new one, how would it, you know...”}",
40: "{P:“That is, what it will cost?”}",
41: "{A:“Yes.”}",
42: "{P:“Well, you'll have to lay out a hundred and fifty, and a bit more,”} said Petrovich, and pressed his lips together meaningfully. He was very fond of strong effects, fond of suddenly bewildering a man completely and then glancing sidelong to see what sort of face the bewildered man would make after such words.",
43: "{A:“A hundred and fifty roubles for an overcoat!”} cried poor Akaky Akakievich, cried perhaps for the first time in his life, for he had always been distinguished by the quietness of his voice.",
44: "{P:“Yes, sir,”} said Petrovich, {P:“and what an overcoat, too. If you put marten on the collar and add a hood with a silk lining, it will run to two hundred.”}",
45: "{A:“Petrovich, please,”} said Akaky Akakievich in a pleading voice, not hearing and not trying to hear the words Petrovich said and all his effects, {A:“mend it somehow, so that it will serve a little longer.”}",
46: "{P:“No, that won't do: it would be killing the work and wasting money,”} said Petrovich, and after such words Akaky Akakievich went out utterly crushed. And Petrovich, after he had gone, stood a long time with his lips meaningfully pressed together, not taking up his work, pleased that he had neither lowered himself nor betrayed the tailor's art.",
47: "Out in the street, Akaky Akakievich was as in a dream. {A:“So that is how the matter stands,”} he said to himself. {A:“I really never thought that it would turn out like that...”} and then, after some silence, he added: {A:“So that's how it is! This is what has come of it at last, and I really could not have supposed that it would be so.”} After this followed another long silence, after which he said: {A:“So that's how! It really is something quite unexpected, you know... I never could have... what a circumstance!”} Having said this, instead of going home, he went in the quite opposite direction, without suspecting it. On the way a chimney sweep brushed against him with his whole dirty side and blackened his whole shoulder; a whole hatful of lime fell on him from the top of a house being built.",
48: "He noticed none of this, and only afterwards, when he bumped into a watchman who, having set his halberd beside him, was shaking some snuff out of a horn onto his calloused fist, only then did he come round a little, and only because the watchman said: {M:“What are you shoving into my very face for, haven't you got the pavement?”} This made him look round and turn homewards. Only here did he begin to collect his thoughts, saw his position in a clear and true light, began to talk with himself no longer in fragments but reasonably and frankly, as with a sensible friend with whom one can discuss the most heartfelt and intimate matter.",
49: "{A:“Well, no,”} said Akaky Akakievich, {A:“it is no use talking to Petrovich now: he is, you know... his wife must have given him a beating. But I had better come to him on Sunday morning: after Saturday night he will be squinting and sleepy, so he will need something to put him right, and his wife will not give him money, and at that moment I will slip him a ten-kopeck piece, you know, into his hand, and he will be more agreeable, and the overcoat then, you know...”} So reasoned Akaky Akakievich with himself, took heart, waited for the first Sunday, and, seeing from afar that Petrovich's wife had gone out of the house somewhere, went straight to him. Petrovich was indeed squinting badly after Saturday, held his head towards the floor and was quite sleepy; but for all that, as soon as he learned what the matter was, it was as if the devil had given him a push.",
50: "{P:“Impossible,”} he said. {P:“Be so good as to order a new one.”} Akaky Akakievich slipped the ten-kopeck piece into his hand right then. {P:“Thank you, sir, I'll fortify myself a little to your health,”} said Petrovich, {P:“and as for the overcoat, don't trouble yourself: it is good for nothing at all. I shall make you a new overcoat that is a glory, we'll stand on that.”}",
51: "Akaky Akakievich tried once more about mending, but Petrovich did not hear and said: {P:“A new one I shall certainly sew for you, you may rely on that, we shall put in the effort. We could even, as the fashion has come in, have the collar fasten with silver clasps under appliqué.”}",
}
